1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
This answers questions about US federal employment regulation.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:11,000
That sounds narrow. Here is why it isn't: the law changes.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,500
The same question, asked about two different dates, has two
different correct answers.

4
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:23,000
This paragraph was amended. Both readings are right — one of
them in 2024, the other one now.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Most retrieval systems give you one of those and never tell you
which one you got.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,500
Now watch the order things arrive in.

7
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:40,000
Nine thousand four hundred paragraphs were eligible. Sixteen
came back, cited and readable, in under half a second.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,000
The written answer takes about six more seconds, because a
language model is generating it.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Most systems put a spinner over both and show you nothing.
This one gives you the law first.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,500
If you read regulation for a living, the citation is the answer.
The paragraph is what you act on.

11
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:03,000
Now a follow-up — and notice the date is pinned to the
question above.

12
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,500
Ask about 2019, then say "and what about the exception".
A normal chatbot has to guess which year you meant.

13
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:14,500
Guess wrong and it quotes repealed law with total confidence.

14
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:20,000
So this endpoint has no date parameter at all. Not "we remember
to pass it through" — there is no way to send a different one.

15
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,000
The conversation survives a reload, and the link can be sent
to someone else.

16
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,000
And every stage recorded what it saw and what it cost.

17
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
So when the answer is wrong — and sometimes it will be — the
question isn't "why did the AI hallucinate".

18
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,000
It's which stage lost the evidence. That has an answer.

19
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
One last thing. Five components in here are switched off:
a reranker, an entailment verifier, a calibrated confidence model,

20
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
multi-hop retrieval, and a fine-tuned encoder. Each is built,
tested, and disabled.

21
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Each one has the p-value that decided it sitting next to the switch.
I measured them and could not show they helped.

22
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Shipping them on anyway would have been the easy version
of this project.

23
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Code, every measurement, and the ones that came out negative —
the link is below.
